ired as an SPI bus: A0/~CS (pin 10) to CS1/CS2, A1/SI (pin 11) to SERIAL_COPI, SO (pin 12) to SERIAL_CIPO, and SCLK (pin 13) to SERIAL_CLK.\",\n      \"expected\": \"For SPI operation the I2C/SPI select pin must be strapped to the SPI level (typically VDD). A GND strap disables the SPI interface that the wiring uses.\",\n      \"evidence\": [\n        {\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(node (ref \\\"U13\\\") (pin \\\"9\\\") (pinfunction \\\"I2C/~{SPI}\\\") (pintype \\\"input\\\"))\"},\n        {\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(node (ref \\\"U13\\\") (pin \\\"10\\\") (pinfunction \\\"A0/~{CS}\\\") (pintype \\\"input\\\"))\"},\n        {\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(node (ref \\\"U14\\\") (pin \\\"9\\\") (pinfunction \\\"I2C/~{SPI}\\\") (pintype \\\"input\\\"))\"}\n      ],\n      \"impact\": \"If the strap is wrong, both UART bridges may not respond to the CM4's SPI transactions, cutting off the serial/SBUS-related channels.\",\n      \"confidence\": 0.35\n    }\n  ],\n  \"summary\": \"The netlist shows an e-stop indicator LED channel (D30) whose anode is tied to the ESTOP_OUT line, so its loop cannot light. Both SC16IS752 UARTs also have their SPI-mode-wired pins combined with an I2C-selecting GND strap; I rate the LED finding higher because the loop topology is more clearly inconsistent.\"\n}",
